      "topic": {
        "text": "Using the ternary operator to assign a class name based on income",
        "refs": ["Q0000", "Q0001", "Q0002"]
      },
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "First give the variable an initial value (e.g., \"economy\") and then use a ternary expression: if the income is greater than $100,000, the expression returns \"first\"; otherwise it returns \"economy\". This replaces the if‑else statement and eliminates the else clause.",
        "refs": ["Q0000", "Q0001", "Q0002"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null
    },
    {
      "topic": {
        "text": "Structure of the ternary operator in Java",
        "refs": ["Q0002", "Q0003"]
      },
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "The ternary operator consists of three parts: a condition, a question mark, the value to return if the condition is true, a colon, and the value to return if the condition is false. It can replace an if‑statement that assigns a value based on a boolean condition.",
        "refs": ["Q0002", "Q0003"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null
    },
    {
      "topic": {
        "text": "Switch statements as an alternative to if statements for role checking",
        "refs": ["Q0004", "Q0005"]
      },
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "A switch statement can execute different code blocks depending on the value of an expression, similar to if statements. For example, checking a string variable \"role\" against values like \"admin\" can be done with a switch to print role‑specific messages.",
        "refs": ["Q0004", "Q0005"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "exemplifies": {
        "text": "Assigning class name based on income using ternary operator",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      },
      "example_text": {
        "text": "String className = (income > 100000) ? \"first\" : \"economy\";",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      },
      "example_type": null
    },
    {
      "exemplifies": {
        "text": "Checking user role with if statement",
        "refs": ["Q0004"]
      },
      "example_text": {
        "text": "if (role.equals(\"admin\")) {\n    System.out.println(\"You are an admin\");\n}",
        "refs": ["Q0004"]
      },
      "example_type": null
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "In Java, the ternary operator is written as condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse.",
        "refs": ["Q0002", "Q0003"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "A string variable cannot be used on the left side of a boolean expression; instead, a ternary operator can assign a string based on a boolean condition.",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    }
